1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,349
Let's put all of Track A together on something real: working with a codebase, end to end.

2
00:00:03,349 --> 00:00:07,000
Files tool, working directory, and the prompting patterns — all at once, on an actual repository.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,050
Start by setting the working directory to a real project folder — that's the boundary from Episode 9.

4
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:13,350
Switch on the Files tools.

5
00:00:13,350 --> 00:00:16,000
Now the agent can read this repo, and only this repo.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,834
First, understand unfamiliar code — but scope the question.

7
00:00:18,834 --> 00:00:21,332
Not “explain this repo,” which wastes a dozen reads.

8
00:00:21,332 --> 00:00:23,205
Instead: “How does authentication work?

9
00:00:23,205 --> 00:00:24,502
Start from the login route.

10
00:00:24,502 --> 00:00:27,000
” A specific entry point keeps it focused and cheap.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:28,186
Next, review a recent change.

12
00:00:28,186 --> 00:00:32,482
Ask it to look at the latest commit or a diff and tell you what changed and whether anything looks risky.

13
00:00:32,482 --> 00:00:36,000
Notice it reads exactly the files it needs and shows you each one in a tool-call card.

14
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,967
Now make a small, contained edit — one file, one clear change.

15
00:00:38,967 --> 00:00:43,273
Watch the write show up as a tool-call card you can inspect and verify before trusting it.

16
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:46,000
Small and contained means easy to check and easy to undo.

17
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,426
For anything bigger — a change across several files — use the plan-before-work pattern from Episode 13.

18
00:00:50,426 --> 00:00:52,875
Ask for the plan first, read it, correct it, then say go.

19
00:00:52,875 --> 00:00:57,000
Fixing a wrong plan costs a sentence; fixing wrong edits across five files costs your afternoon.

20
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The gotcha the docs themselves call out: vague requests cause wasted exploration.

21
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,519
“Fix the bug” sends it reading half the repo to guess what you mean.

22
00:01:02,519 --> 00:01:04,037
Name the file, the function, the symptom.

23
00:01:04,037 --> 00:01:07,000
Specificity is the difference between a focused agent and an expensive wanderer.

24
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,141
That's Track A — you can install it, connect a model, and put a capable agent to work on real files.

25
00:01:10,141 --> 00:01:12,372
Track B goes intermediate, starting with voice: actually talking to it.

26
00:01:12,372 --> 00:01:13,000
See you in Module 5.

